Let there be as many units in {D} as the times that {C} measures {A}, and as many units in {E} as the times that {C} measures {B}.

Since {C} measures {A} according to the units in {D}, therefore {C} multiplied by {D} makes {A}. For the same reason {C} multiplied by {E} makes {B}. [!just VII.Def.15]

Thus the number {C} multiplied by the two numbers {D} and {E} makes {A} and {B}, therefore {D} is to {E} as {A} is to {B}. [!just VII.17]

Therefore {D} and {E} are in the same ratio with {A} and {B}, being less than they, which is impossible. Therefore no number measures the numbers {A} and {B}.

Therefore {A} and {B} are relatively prime.

Therefore, *the least numbers of those which have the same ratio with them are relatively prime.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition is the converse of the last one. Together they say that a ratio *a* : *b* is reduced to lowest terms if and only if *a* is relatively prime to *b.*

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used in propositions @VIII.2, @VIII.3, and @IX.15.
